{# Drupal's local-task row. `local_tasks_block` builds primary and secondary;
   this template prints those children so the renderer applies each task's
   `#access`, passing each into `bb_theme:tabs`' `tab_items` slot; each
   remaining item is `menu-local-task.html.twig`, rendering `bb_theme:tab`.
   Storybook and scenes use the same component through its `items` prop; this
   presenter never rebuilds that list.

   `secondary` is a distinct row. Drupal puts a second level beneath the first;
   flattening it into the primary row would misrepresent the hierarchy.

   `[data-tabs]` is the Playwright hook for the row (`ticket-detail.steps.ts`).

   THIS ROW RIDES ALONG INSIDE `island-content`, AND POSITION IS THE WHOLE
   MECHANISM (BB-396 D8). `local_tasks_block` is placed as a prefix of the
   `page_content` island, after the heading and before the body. So when a tab is clicked
   and the client narrows the response to that island, this row is inside the
   replaced markup and its current entry is right afterwards — without
   declaring anything of its own. That is why there is no htmx attribute in
   this template and why adding one would be a defect rather than an
   improvement: `IslandBoost` is the single author of a declaration, and a
   second one here would fight the container that already carries it.

   THE RAIL IS THE CONTRAST, and it is a deliberate asymmetry (D3, module fact
   F6). `island-rail` names the same swap target, `#island-content`, but sits
   OUTSIDE it, so it is not part of what a swap replaces and its active entry
   may go stale. Making it ride along would mean rendering the whole menu tree
   on every navigation — the exact cost islands exist to avoid. These tabs get
   a correct current entry for free because they are inside the boundary; the
   rail pays nothing and accepts a stale trail. #}
{% if primary %}
  {{ include('bb_theme:tabs', {
    attributes: attributes,
    label: 'Sections'|t,
    tab_items: primary,
  }, with_context = false) }}
{% endif %}

{% if secondary %}
  {{ include('bb_theme:tabs', {
    label: 'Subsections'|t,
    tab_items: secondary,
  }, with_context = false) }}
{% endif %}
